    <div class="measure">
      <h2>Holding 100 square meters flat</h2>
      <p>The scanner uses an X-axis linear slide, built from two modular sections, on six floor-mounted masts, leveled as one unit. A Y-axis linear slide rides on that and carries the Z-roll unit.</p>
      <p>The corrections are doing the work. MV-Cor corrects X, Y, and Z position as a function of (X, Y), which is what holds Z planarity across the scan plane, and the roll axis is aligned to the scan plane. A Leica AT960-LR laser tracker handles alignment and surface measurements of the reflectors. Tracker data also perturbed ideal positions so those position errors could be pushed through the transform.</p>
      <p>The DUT side is just as practical. A telescoping mast (3 to 6 m) handles payloads up to 440 lbs, with a Symmetrie BREVA hexapod for fine alignment: ±0.5 μm in X, Y, and Z, and ±2.5 μrad in rotation. For a larger DUT the mast comes off and the payload sits on the floor. The facility takes payloads up to 1320 lbs, including a lunar rover or a UAV.</p>

      <h2>One RF system, 1 to 110 GHz</h2>
      <p>Below 50 GHz, the system runs a dual-PXIe architecture: one Keysight M9010A chassis on the scanner and one at the DUT, each with M9808A VNA modules. Shielded enclosures sit next to both chassis. Operation from 1 to 50 GHz breaks into 1 to 20 GHz and 20 to 50 GHz. For 20 to 50 GHz, a 4x multiplier takes the LO from a Keysight MXG in a ground rack, and the reference path is down-converted to a 31 MHz IF.</p>
      <p>From 50 to 110 GHz, VDI frequency extender modules cover V, E and W bands. Probes are dual linear through Q band. In V, E, and W they are single linear.</p>
      <p>Calibration traceability was built in from the start. Probes were calibrated at UPM for boresight gain, and the near-field to far-field transform uses simulated probe patterns from MV-Plane. Every reference standard gain horn came with a boresight gain calibration at NPL.</p>
      <p>Dynamic range is reported by band, not as one number. At 1 kHz IFBW the typical spans are 97 to 100 dB in L and S, 110 to 80 dB in C, 80 to 70 dB in X, 90 to 80 dB in Ku, 75 to 60 dB in Ka, 90 to 65 dB in Q, and 75 to 82 dB in V. E band is 82 to 91 dB and W band is 60 to 80 dB, both at 100 Hz IFBW. Receiver dynamic range was measured at boresight.</p>
    </div>

