    <div class="measure">
      <h2>More than patterns</h2>
      <p>Because the payloads include active antennas, the range supports special test modes beyond gain and patterns:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>EIRP:</strong> a near-field scan, then post-processing, with an integrated power meter capturing input power</li>
        <li><strong>G/T:</strong> the CW-ambient method, using PXIe spectrum analyzer mode with band noise markers, then post-processing with calibrated power levels</li>
        <li><strong>IMD:</strong> swept IMD, with an RF combiner in the enclosure from 1 to 50 GHz and dual-source VDI modules from 50 to 110 GHz</li>
        <li><strong>Gain compression:</strong> input power sweeps to identify the compression point</li>
        <li><strong>Pulsed RF:</strong> asynchronous narrowband, wideband, and pulse profiling</li>
        <li><strong>Park and probe:</strong> the probe parks on an X-Y coordinate, measures one DUT state, then moves to the next, with external scripts for phased-array calibration</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Does the uncertainty budget match reality?</h2>
      <p>This is the part I would point any range engineer to.</p>
      <p>We ran an uncertainty analysis from the semi-rigorous 18-term budget in IEEE Std 1720, at analysis frequencies across the bands. Each term was evaluated by analysis or measurement: probe calibration data from UPM, laser tracker data used to perturb ideal data and push it through the transform, quarter-wavelength probe and DUT offsets for multiple reflections and room scattering, boresight roll and attenuator checks for linearity, drift tests, and back-to-back repeatability. Where the table reports it, main beam 2σ uncertainty runs from ±0.230 dB to ±0.519 dB. L band and Q band are blank in that column.</p>
      <p>Then we checked it against measurement. At each band, five measurements were taken at 1/8-wavelength Z steps. The mean of the five became the "truth," and the worst-case 3σ within the 3 dB main beam became the gain accuracy metric. The idea is simple: a 3σ (99.7%) spread from real data should fall inside the 2σ (95.4%) interval from the analysis. Measured main-beam 3σ values run from ±0.092 dB to ±0.348 dB. In every band where both numbers were reported, the measured 3σ sat inside the analyzed 2σ.</p>
    </div>

